===Etymology===
From {{compound|snake|fly|lang=en}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|snakeflies}}

# Any insect of the order [[Raphidioptera]].
#* '''1997''', Charles S. Henry, ''11: Modern mating systems in archaic Holometabola: sexuality in neuropterid insects'', Jae C. Choe, Bernard J. Crespi (editors), ''The Evolution of Mating Systems in Insects and Arachnids'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=X3Iz-zXolVcC&pg=PA196&dq=%22snakefly%22%7C%22snakeflies%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=KdaIUuXiKYH_iAfHw4DQDg&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22snakefly%22%7C%22snakeflies%22&f=false page 196],
#*: Although sexual behavior is not well studied in the order, observations exist for members of both '''snakefly''' families.
#* '''2009''', David Hugh Kattes, ''Insects of Texas: a Practical Guide'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=BRCBQwjhC8kC&pg=PA109&dq=%22snakefly%22%7C%22snakeflies%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=KdaIUuXiKYH_iAfHw4DQDg&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22snakefly%22%7C%22snakeflies%22&f=false page 109],
#*: Adult '''snakeflies''' stalk their prey in a manner similar to snakes. When an insect comes within reach, the '''snakefly''' will strike, catching the victim with its jaws.
#* '''2012''', Jogeir N. Stokland, Juha Siitonen, Bengt Gunnar Jonsson, ''Biodiversity in Dead Wood'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=3R4i5HKMfxEC&pg=PA239&dq=%22snakefly%22%7C%22snakeflies%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=KdaIUuXiKYH_iAfHw4DQDg&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22snakefly%22%7C%22snakeflies%22&f=false page 239],
#*: The order '''snakeflies''' (Raphidioptera) consists predominantly of saproxylic predators (Aspöck, 2002). '''Snakeflies''' have an almost worldwide distribution, mostly occurring in temperate regions and in mountainous areas closer to the equator.

